---
// design/03_SECTORS.md, ui_ux/03_SECTORS_UI.md.
//
// REBUILT 2026-09-06 as an index, following the case-studies reference: a banner, then a grid of
// dark cards, each opening its own page. Was five stacked full-width panels carrying every
// sector's complete detail on one route — the detail now lives on /sectors/[id].
//
// The reference puts a red date badge in each card's corner. These have no dates, so the badge
// carries the entry-order number instead — which is real information here: design/03 is explicit
// below. Rebuilt 2026-09-07: the entry-order badge is now the market's AXIS (`12 §1`) — whether
// it segments by industry, by obligation, or by scale — which is a permanent property of the
// market rather than a position in a release queue.
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro';
import PageBanner from '../components/PageBanner.astro';
import GetInTouch from '../components/GetInTouch.astro';
import SectorGraphic from '../components/SectorGraphic.astro';
import { sectors } from '../data/sectors';
---

<Page title="Five sectors, one architecture" description={"Every sector that holds data asks the same question in its own vocabulary: what did this model take from it, and can that be proven? These five are where the answer is worth the most."}>
	<PageBanner
		eyebrow="FIVE MARKETS"
		title="Every sector that holds data has this problem. These five are where solving it is worth the most."
		trail={[{ label: 'Home', href: '/' }, { label: 'Sectors' }]}
	/>

	<section class="section container sectors-head">
		<p class="sectors-intro">
			Data is the common thread. A bank, a hospital, a training lab, a data controller and a
			defence programme all need to know what a model took from their data and why. Each calls the
			question something different, whether validation, audit, diagnosis, erasure or assurance,
			and each is told the same thing by their current stack. The answer cannot be recovered after
			training. It cannot. It has to be produced during it.
		</p>

		<p class="sectors-order-note">
			So these are not five products, five solutions or five versions of anything. They are five
			readings of one record, under one licence. What differs between them is the obligation
			driving the purchase, the person who signs, and how long the sale takes. Plenty of other
			sectors hold data under the same constraint and the same licence reaches them. These five
			are named because the cost of the missing account is already on somebody's books there, in
			figures that can be checked.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="section-tight container reveal">
		<div class="case-grid">
			{sectors.map((sector) => (
				<a href={`/sectors/${sector.id}`} class="case-card">
					<div class="case-card-top">
						<span class="case-card-tag">Market</span>
						<span class="case-card-axis">{sector.axis}</span>
					</div>

					<h2 class="case-card-title">{sector.name}</h2>
					<p class="case-card-badge">{sector.question}</p>

					<div class="case-card-art">
						<SectorGraphic id={sector.id} />
					</div>

					<span class="case-card-cta">
						Read the market
						<span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
					</span>
				</a>
			))}
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section container reveal">
		<p class="sectors-pricing-note">
			ORMAS is one licence. Terms are agreed per institution and written into the contract, and
			there is no price list.
		</p>
	</section>

	<GetInTouch variant="other" heading="Which of the five applies?" />
</Page>
